prose.text:
Before, `beside` stored the finished rows and a row-to-path map in it, and `paint` recoloured those rows. Now it stores the tree's inputs (`files`, `parts`, `width`), so each paint can build rows for the window it has. Who writes it and who reads it:

{{diagram:sheetside}}

`parts` is typed inline here and as `thread.SidePart[]` in `side.lua`: the same shape, written twice. `width` is always `side.WIDTH`, set by `beside`, so nothing can yet draw the tree at another width.

It returns `thread.SideRow`s:

{{chunk:side-row-class}}

No rule: `fit` adds it. With `fold`, a directory none of whose files is lit is one row. `lit` changes the colours as well as the shape, so the same files give different rows from one item to the next.

introduction.text:
`side.fit(side, lit, height)` (#contract:5) turns the tree into at most `height` rows. `lit` is the set of files the item under the cursor touches: a step's chunk file, a tour's files, a contract's. The column is the rule, then the tree whole, folded, or a stretch of it with a flag at each edge it is cut at:

{{diagram:fit}}

The stretch is where the edge cases are: #step:3 picks where it starts, #step:4 sizes it around its flags, #step:5 draws them.

The tree used to be drawn once, from the sheet's first row down. Now every paint draws it from the window's first row (or the sheet's, while that is still in view) and only as far as the window's last row, so it stays put as the main column scrolls:

{{diagram:pin}}

`beside` still pads the sheet by the unfolded tree's height (#step:9), from before the tree could fit itself.

text.text:
`WinScrolled` matches on the window id, so the callback compares it with `s.win`, and it skips while `M.draw` runs. Moving the cursor already repaints, through `CursorMoved`:

{{chunk:screen-cursormoved}}

So `j` on the window's last row, which moves the cursor and scrolls, paints twice, and a draw that scrolls the window paints once at the end of `M.draw` and once here. Each paint builds the tree (#note:9).

introduction.text:
`M.tree` (#contract:6) replaces `M.rows` (#contract:3): the same walk, directories first with a chain of lone ones on one row, then the files set in under them. What changed is that a row now carries its path and whether it is lit, and is built with `lit` in hand. The lighting moves from `paint`, which recoloured finished rows, into the rows:

{{diagram:calls}}

The one visible change outside folding and flags is that directory rows now fade too (#step:13), which the surface test records.

prose.text:
#step:13 fades every directory once anything is lit, and #step:15 shows what that looks like: `skills/answering-threads/` faded right above the lit `show.lua`. How many lit files a directory holds is already counted a few lines up:

{{chunk:side-tree-fold}}

so `(lit and on == 0) and 'ThreadSideFaded' or 'ThreadMeta'` would keep the path down to each lit file readable and fade only the rest. In the surface test, the rows of `lua/thread/`, `model/`, `skills/answering-threads/` and `tests/` (2, 11, 19 and 22) would go back to `ThreadMeta`.
